  "examples": [
   {
    "key": "Etto",
    "name": "Etto",
    "place": "Paso Robles, California",
    "story": "Pasta is not just a vehicle for sauce. Etto makes it in Paso Robles from organic American-grown durum wheat, pushing the dough through bronze dies and drying it slowly. The rough surface holds onto what matters: the sauce you took the trouble to cook.",
    "url": "https://ettopastificio.com/pages/about-us"
   },
   {
    "key": "Fair Hills Farm",
    "name": "Fair Hills Farm",
    "place": "San Miguel, California",
    "story": "An organic orchard in San Miguel, turning a harvest into more than a pretty piece of fruit. Fair Hills presses apples into unfiltered juice and uses orchard apple juice to gently sweeten its teas. The point is the fruit: grown with care, carried through into the bottle.",
    "url": "https://www.fairhillsfarmorganic.com/thefarm"
   },
   {
    "key": "Linn's",
    "name": "Linn's",
    "place": "Cambria, California",
    "story": "Linn’s story starts with a family and a Central Coast farm. They have been growing crops and making food since 1979, with the next generation still working the land. These preserves belong on real bread, with real butter. A modest breakfast can be a very good meal.",
    "url": "https://www.linnseats.com/our-farm"
   },
   {
    "key": "Cal Poly",
    "name": "Cal Poly",
    "place": "San Luis Obispo, California",
    "story": "At Cal Poly, food is something you learn by making. Students work on products from cheese and chocolate to jam and barbecue sauce. This is San Luis Obispo’s hands-on food tradition: practical skills, real ingredients, and something you can actually bring home for dinner.",
    "url": "https://fsn.calpoly.edu/our-products"
   },
   {
    "key": "Joebella / Avocado Shack",
    "name": "Joebella / Avocado Shack",
    "place": "Atascadero, California",
    "story": "Joseph and Isabel built Joebella around a shared devotion to coffee. They roast small batches of organic beans in Atascadero, where the café and roasting facility are certified organic. The Shack’s coffee selection puts that nearby craft within reach of your own morning routine.",
    "url": "https://www.joebellacoffee.com/our-story"
   },
   {
    "key": "Sunshine Olive",
    "name": "Sunshine Olive",
    "place": "Templeton, California",
    "story": "Sunshine makes extra virgin olive oil in California, with its home in Templeton. The photographed bottles name the estate and olive variety—a useful reminder that olive oil has an origin and a character. Give it warm bread, a ripe tomato, or vegetables straight from the pan.",
    "url": "https://sunshineoliveoil.com/"
   },
   {
    "key": "Massa Organics",
    "name": "Massa Organics",
    "place": "Near Chico, California",
    "story": "Greg Massa and Raquel Krach farm near Chico in the Sacramento Valley, growing organic rice and almonds while working to build healthier soil. Their medium-grain brown rice is the sort of staple worth getting right: something steady underneath vegetables, beans, or the last good spoonful of sauce.",
    "url": "https://massaorganics.com/get-to-know-us/"
   },
   {
    "key": "AG Berries",
    "name": "AG Berries",
    "place": "Arroyo Grande, California",
    "story": "Maria “Trini” Iñiguez founded Arroyo Grande Berry Farm in 2016, carrying forward her brother’s dream of organic farming. The work is personal. So is the pleasure of a good berry: ripe fruit, a little acidity, and no great need to turn it into anything else.",
    "url": "https://agberryfarm.com/"
   },
   {
    "key": "Stardust Grove",
    "name": "Stardust Grove",
    "place": "Cambria, California",
    "story": "Stardust Grove is a family farm in Cambria with a regenerative approach to caring for its orchard. The tubs on the Shack’s shelves bring that farm name into everyday cooking. Start with something simple: chips and guacamole, or a spoonful of pesto through a pot of pasta.",
    "url": "https://thestardustgrove.com/"
   },
   {
    "key": "Maple Valley",
    "name": "Maple Valley",
    "place": "Cashton, Wisconsin",
    "story": "Not everything good has to come from down the road. Maple Valley is a cooperative with its bottling operation in Wisconsin, bringing organic maple syrup from its producers to the table. Dark and robust means a proper maple presence—on breakfast, in baking, or alongside something savory.",
    "url": "https://www.maplevalleysyrup.coop/about-maple-valley-cooperative/"
   },
   {
    "key": "Cayucos",
    "name": "Cayucos",
    "place": "Cayucos, California",
    "story": "Cayucos Hot Sauce was born in a little shack across from the beach on Highway 1. A Central Coast sauce with an obvious place at the table: eggs, tacos, seafood, or whatever needs a little more life. Heat should make you want the next bite.",
    "url": "https://cayucoshotsauce.com/pages/about-us"
   },
   {
    "key": "Jayleaf",
    "name": "Jayleaf",
    "place": "Hollister, California",
    "story": "Jayleaf is a family greens business with roots in Hollister. Its work centers on leafy greens, individual varieties and carefully composed mixes. Fresh leaves are not decoration: they are the meal’s sharp, green counterpoint to warm bread, rich cheese, or something just off the grill.",
    "url": "https://www.jayleaf.com/our-story"
   },
   {
    "key": "Love Beets",
    "name": "Love Beets",
    "place": "Rochester, New York",
    "story": "Love Beets grew out of Guy and Katherine Shropshire’s family interest in making beets easier to enjoy. The company put down production roots in Rochester, New York, in 2016. A reminder that a familiar ingredient can still surprise you when someone gives it a little attention.",
    "url": "https://lovebeets.com/our-story/"
   }
  ],
